INT. RIVERBOAT CASINO COURTROOM - DAY

A gambling hall converted into a temporary courtroom after the flood. Slot machines are draped in canvas, and brown water stains climb the walls. A judge's bench has been built from a roulette table.

CLEMENTINE ASHWORTH, the defense attorney, stands at a card table with her hands folded. She is sharp, silver-haired, and never hurried. Across from her, MAXWELL DRUMM, the prosecutor, flips through a damp stack of papers. He is young and eager, and his collar is two sizes too big. At the roulette bench, JUDGE ISADORA FENWICK adjusts her glasses and glares down at both of them. Near the door, BAILIFF NORBERT KWASNIEWSKI shifts his weight, his hand resting on a holstered flashlight.

JUDGE FENWICK
Mr. Drumm. You were saying.

He clears his throat and rises, dropping a page. He stoops, recovers it, and comes up red.

MAXWELL
Your Honor, the State maintains the defendant knowingly opened the sluice gates.

CLEMENTINE
Objection. He has not yet shown a single witness who saw her touch them.

JUDGE FENWICK
Sustained. Move along.

Maxwell glances at his notes, then at the bailiff, who gives him nothing. He swallows.

MAXWELL
Then I call the night watchman.

Norbert opens the door. No one is there. He steps out, comes back, and shrugs.

NORBERT
He ain't come, ma'am.

JUDGE FENWICK
Of course he hasn't.

Clementine rises slowly, smoothing her skirt. She walks to the center of the room and lets the silence stand.

CLEMENTINE
Your Honor, the State's case is built on a man who is not here, a gate no one saw opened, and a river that has a long habit of rising.

Maxwell opens his mouth, and she raises a hand. He closes it.

CLEMENTINE (CONT'D)
I move to dismiss.

The judge studies her over her glasses, then looks at the stained ceiling, the draped slots, the roulette wheel under her palm. She spins it. It clatters and settles.

JUDGE FENWICK
Red seventeen.

She lifts the gavel.

JUDGE FENWICK (CONT'D)
Dismissed.
